<h2>Compare Fractions — source-checked tasks</h2><p>Estimation Exploration: use the original ladybug with one-inch reference to propose TOO LOW, ABOUT RIGHT and TOO HIGH estimates; no measured image result is fixed.</p>
<h3>Four original insect comparisons</h3><table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Insects</th><th>Distances</th><th>Source-checked result</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>beetle vs caterpillar</td><td>2/8 vs 2/3</td><td>caterpillar</td></tr><tr><td>grasshopper vs caterpillar</td><td>4 centimeters vs 12/3 centimeters</td><td>same length</td></tr><tr><td>ladybug vs ant</td><td>3/8 vs 5/8</td><td>ant</td></tr><tr><td>grasshopper vs frog</td><td>5/8 vs 5/6</td><td>frog</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3>What Fraction Makes Sense?</h3><p>Original spilled-juice expressions have several correct completions. Never assume one exact hidden digit.</p><table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Source expression</th><th>Correct constraint</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>2/8 &lt; n/8</td><td>n&gt;2</td></tr><tr><td>3/6 = unknown fraction</td><td>same value as 1/2</td></tr><tr><td>4/3 &gt; 4/d</td><td>d&gt;3</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Also find a LESS fraction, GREATER fraction, and EQUIVALENT fraction for each of 4/6 and3/4, and explain with a consistent whole; the original supporting student note also shows1/2.</p>
<h3>OPTIONAL Ultimate Locate and Label — eleven original labels, zero to three</h3><p>1/2, 3/8, 13/8, 2/4, 3/4, 9/8, 5/4, 12/6, 5/2, 9/3, 20/8</p><p>Teacher checks position and equality, notably1/2=2/4 and5/2=20/8.</p>
